    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 241744\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1044 AM PDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Hotter than normal temperatures will continue through the week.\n\n* Best chance of storms along and south of I-15 today, with \n  diminishing odds afterwards.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through the weekend.\n\nA slight cooldown is anticipated today and Tuesday thanks to a \nsubtle uptick in moisture and fall in 500mb heights. Highs during \nthis time are forecast to be 1-5 degrees cooler than yesterday \n(largest cooldown across the southeastern half of the CWA), but \nstill above normal for late August. From Wednesday through \nFriday, ensemble guidance suggests an uptick in 500mb heights and \nsouthwesterly flow, the latter yielding a gradual reduction of \nmoisture. The net effect of these changes is warming at the \nsurface, with highs returning to values 3-8 degrees above normal. \nCombined with oppressive overnight lows, major to extreme heatrisk\nresumes across most of the Mojave Desert. Over the weekend (and \ninto early next week), ensembles depict a broader trough \napproaching the western US, leading to cooler temperatures and \nincreased breezes.\n\nOvernight radar and satellite imagery shows a complex of storms in \nsouthern AZ pushing our way. Convection should wane before it \nreaches our area, but the southerly outflow will advect in \nadditional moisture through the early morning hours. Latest HREF \nshows the 1\" PWAT contour extending up to the I-15 corridor this \nafternoon, which should allow isolated to scattered storms in this\narea. Main concerns will be gusty outflow winds and dry \nlightning, but cannot rule out isolated instances of flash \nflooding. Moisture should then gradually decrease throughout the \nweek, leading to more isolated and weaker activity. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds \nwill favor a southwesterly direction and strengthen this afternoon, \nwith speeds in excess of 10 knots and gusts between 20 and 25 knots. \nThere is a low (10 to 20 percent) chance of convection on high \nterrain around the Las Vegas Valley this afternoon. Regional \nthunderstorm activity may produce outflow winds that could cause a \nsudden change in wind direction and intensity throughout the \nafternoon and evening, but confidence is too low to include in the \nTAF at this time. Outside of convective influence, southwesterly \nwinds fall to less than 10 knots then become variable overnight. \nTemperatures exceed 100 degrees through 03Z and increase to over 100 \ndegrees by 19Z tomorrow. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Gusty southerly to \nsouthwesterly winds develop this afternoon in the Las Vegas Valley, \nColorado River Valley, and Owens Valley. Peak gusts should be \nbetween 20 and 25 knots. Isolated to scattered thunderstorm activity \nis expected this afternoon and evening in areas along and southeast \nof Interstate 15, with the highest confidence for impacts at KEED \nand KIFP. The threat of gusty and erratic outflow winds from these \nstorms will last throughout the afternoon and evening. Outside of \nconvective influence, winds should weaken for most areas overnight, \nand light winds will last into tomorrow morning. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
